[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue: the parser reads dates month first, our tenants write the day first, the fix is queued. That is true for most of the reports. It is not true for all of them, and the queued fix will leave some of them open.

<Callout kind="warn">
The ticket is marked known, but it holds two problems. A fix for the parser alone closes the ticket and leaves the second one unreported.
</Callout>

## What is in the ticket

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

Counts are computed from the ticket system export, one report per id (the export pages overlap, so the same report can come twice). The rows add up to the ticket total. Reports the desk read as a one-day move are counted where they are filed, by tenant, and noted under the table.

## Problem 1 · `parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first

This is the problem #4471 describes. A day-first tenant's dates come out with day and month swapped, in the app, in email and in the booking-v2 form. Every tenant on file with a day-first locale is affected. The fix queued on #4471 is the right fix for this row and only for this row. A few of these reports describe a date that moved by one day instead of a swap (the desk lists them in `desk-notes.json`). They are filed here by tenant, as the desk counts them. If they turn out to have their own cause, they are a third problem to split out later.

## Problem 2 · `hint`: booking-v3 tells day-first people to type month first

The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. Day-first tenants' people type what the hint says, and the form reads it their way, so day and month swap again. The symptom is the same as the parser's, so the reports look like duplicates, but the bug is in the form's hint and the parser fix will not touch it. It only appears in the `form` channel, for the tenants on booking-v3.

## Why the merge went wrong

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject line. The two problems share subject lines ("Date is wrong", "Appointment moved by itself"), so subject matching put them in one ticket. The [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) shows the same subject with different causes. Intake's browser guess does not separate them either: the table shows reports tagged `parser` that belong to `hint`, and the other way round.

## What to do

- Keep #4471 for `parser` only, and file `hint` as its own ticket.
- Match duplicates on cause (tenant locale and form), not on subject. A new decision record should replace 0001; an accepted decision is never edited.
- Re-check the counts above after the parser fix ships. Whatever keeps arriving after that is `hint`, or the one-day reports.

